# Project Architecture

Employee Compliance Tracking System — monorepo, **deployed separately** (backend EB, frontend static/EB, expiry job Lambda, RDS, EventBridge→SQS).

Act as a senior engineer on this codebase: follow project docs and rules; prefer minimal, correct diffs over clever abstractions.

## Stack (do not substitute without updating docs)

- **backend/** — NestJS, TypeScript, TypeORM, MySQL, Passport (local + JWT)
- **expiry-job/** — Python; calls NestJS API over HTTP only (never MySQL)
- **frontend/** — SvelteKit, TypeScript

## Before structural or behavioral changes

Read `docs/architecture-decisions.md`. If a change contradicts a decision, update that doc first.

Also consult: `docs/db-schema.md`, `docs/api-doc.md`, `docs/process-flow.md`, `docs/security.md`, `docs/performance.md`.

## Module boundaries

- One concern per file: entity / DTO / service / controller separated
- Config (env, AWS names) in config modules — not in business logic
- Status computation uses **Asia/Colombo** calendar date + `COMPLIANCE_EXPIRING_BUFFER_DAYS` (shared rule in docs)
- Dashboard: live-computed metrics (no pre-aggregation table without updating Decision #10)

## Env files

- `.env.example` at repo root is the full catalog; per-service templates: `backend/.env.example`, etc.
- **User-owned (never modify in agent edits):** `.env`, `.env.production`, `backend/.env`, `expiry-job/.env`, `frontend/.env`
- **Agent may update:** `.env.example`, `backend/.env.example`, and other `*.env.example` templates when adding config keys
- At runtime each service loads only its own `.env`

## Scope discipline

- Minimize diff scope; match existing patterns in the module being edited
- Add regression tests for bug fixes; document non-obvious business rules in `docs/` when decisions change
